 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Flow from the WSGI server to the view (all in `src/tessel/app.py` unless noted):\n\n1. **`Tessel.__call__`** (`app.py:1618`) just delegates to `wsgi_entry`.\n2. **`Tessel.wsgi_entry`** (`app.py:1566`) builds the context with `self.request_context(environ)`, then calls `ctx.push()`.\n3. **`AppContext.push`** (`ctx.py:416`) activates the context and sends `appcontext_pushed`. For a request context it then:\n   - opens the session via `_get_session()`\n   - runs `match_request()` (`ctx.py:405`), which does URL routing and stores `url_rule`/`view_args` or a `routing_exception` on the request.\n4. **`Tessel.run_full_request`** (`app.py:992`) sends `request_started`, then calls the steps below inside a try block. Any exception goes to `route_user_fault`.\n5. **`Tessel.preprocess_request`** (`app.py:1366`) runs the `url_value_preprocessors`, then the `before_request_funcs` for the app and then the bundles. A non-None return short-circuits the view.\n6. **`Tessel.run_view`** (`app.py:966`) raises any stored routing exception, auto-answers `OPTIONS` if applicable, and otherwise calls `self.ensure_sync(self.view_functions[rule.endpoint])(**view_args)`. **That call is the view function.**\n\nAfter the view returns, `finalize_request` runs (`make_response`, then `finalize_reply`, then `request_finished`). The WSGI response is then called, and `ctx.pop()` runs the teardown functions.",
